          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "Your concern about having had two courses of steroids in the year could be a sign of asthma not being well controlled, but the key factor here is inhaler technique and adherence. Guidance emphasizes that before a clinician considers stepping up treatment, they should check inhaler technique and adherence, as issues with these can significantly affect treatment effectiveness. Without addressing these factors, any increase in medication might just treat the wrong problem (source: guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nWhen it comes to stepping up asthma treatment, the approach is based on the current symptom control and exacerbation history. Treatment can be adjusted in several ways, starting from lower steps (like as-needed medications) to higher steps which may include higher-dose combination therapies. Adjustments are made after reviewing symptom control rather than applying a fixed regimen (source: guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\nIf you have specific questions about your health, it would be best to discuss them directly with your healthcare provider. I could not cover personal medication options or specific mortality risks associated with stepping up treatment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
